VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Two-class method for participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Halcyon Corp. has 200,000 common shares outstanding and 50,000 shares of participating preferred stock that shares equally in dividends above $2.00 per preferred share. Net income is $900,000. Under ASC 260, which method must Halcyon use to compute basic EPS?', '["The two-class method, allocating undistributed earnings between common and participating preferred", "The treasury stock method, treating preferred shares as potential common shares", "The if-converted method, adding preferred dividends back to the numerator", "The standard single-class method, deducting only declared preferred dividends from net income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, when an entity has participating securities that share in undistributed earnings, it must use the two-class method to compute basic EPS. This method allocates undistributed earnings to common stock and participating securities based on their respective participation rights. The treasury stock method (choice B) applies to stock options and warrants, not participating preferred stock.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Simple capital structure definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what is a simple capital structure?', '["A structure with only common stock and convertible bonds outstanding", "A structure with only common stock and no potentially dilutive securities outstanding", "A structure with common stock and noncumulative preferred stock only", "A structure with fewer than three classes of equity securities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a simple capital structure exists when an entity has only common stock outstanding and no potentially dilutive securities such as convertible debt, convertible preferred stock, options, or warrants. An entity with a simple capital structure reports only basic EPS. Choice A is incorrect because convertible bonds are potentially dilutive securities, making the structure complex.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Actual mid-year conversion of bonds effect on diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Mercer Holdings had $1,000,000 of 6% convertible bonds outstanding on January 1, convertible into 30,000 common shares. All bonds were converted on April 1. Mercer had 200,000 common shares on January 1 (before conversion) and a 25% tax rate. In computing diluted EPS, the controller included the 30,000 converted shares only from April 1 and made no interest add-back. Evaluate the controller''s approach.', '["The controller''s approach is correct because actual conversion eliminates any dilutive effect", "The controller should include converted shares from April 1 but must add back 3 months of after-tax interest", "The controller should assume conversion from January 1, adding back 3 months of after-tax interest to the numerator and including 30,000 shares for the full year in the denominator", "The controller should assume conversion from January 1 but make no interest add-back since the bonds no longer exist"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, when convertible securities are actually converted during the period, diluted EPS assumes conversion occurred at the beginning of the period. The numerator adds back after-tax interest for the pre-conversion period ($1,000,000 x 6% x 3/12 x 0.75 = $11,250), and the denominator includes all 30,000 shares from January 1. Choice B incorrectly retains the basic EPS weighting while adding interest back, mismatching numerator and denominator periods.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Potentially dilutive securities definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What are potentially dilutive securities under ASC 260?', '["Securities that increase earnings per share when included in the EPS calculation", "Securities that reduce the entity''s total stockholders'' equity", "Securities that have no effect on the EPS calculation", "Securities that could reduce earnings per share if exercised, converted, or otherwise resulted in the issuance of common shares"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, potentially dilutive securities are instruments such as convertible bonds, convertible preferred stock, stock options, and warrants that, if exercised or converted, could decrease EPS or increase the loss per share. Choice A describes the opposite effect—securities that increase EPS are antidilutive and are excluded from diluted EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — EPS for discontinued operations and control number
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Dunmore Inc. reports income from continuing operations of $800,000 and a loss from discontinued operations of $200,000 (net of tax), yielding net income of $600,000. Dunmore has 200,000 weighted-average common shares and dilutive stock options adding 10,000 incremental shares. How should EPS be presented on Dunmore''s income statement?', '["Basic and diluted EPS must be presented for both continuing operations and net income; the dilution test uses income from continuing operations as the control number", "EPS is presented only for net income; separate EPS for continuing operations and discontinued operations is optional", "Diluted EPS for discontinued operations must use its own dilution test, independent of continuing operations", "Basic EPS is required for all line items, but diluted EPS is only required for net income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, an entity must present basic and diluted EPS for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement. Per ASC 260-10-45-19, the dilutive or antidilutive determination uses income from continuing operations as the control number. Choice B is incorrect because separate EPS for continuing operations is mandatory, not optional.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Post-year-end stock dividend retroactive adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Bellmore Corp. had 300,000 common shares outstanding throughout 2025. On February 15, 2026, before the 2025 financial statements were issued, Bellmore declared and distributed a 20% stock dividend. The 2025 basic EPS computation should use weighted-average shares of:', '["300,000 shares with no adjustment until the 2026 financial statements", "360,000 shares, but only for diluted EPS", "300,000 shares adjusted to 360,000 shares retroactively for both basic and diluted EPS", "300,000 shares with disclosure of the stock dividend in the notes only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, if a stock dividend or stock split occurs after the reporting period but before financial statements are issued, the weighted-average shares for all periods presented must be retroactively adjusted. Bellmore''s 300,000 shares are adjusted by the 20% stock dividend factor (300,000 x 1.20 = 360,000) for the 2025 EPS computation. Choice A is incorrect because retroactive adjustment is required, not deferred to the following year.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Restricted stock units dilution analysis
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Garfield Technologies grants 40,000 RSUs on January 1, 2025, with a 3-year cliff vesting period. Each RSU entitles the holder to one common share upon vesting. As of December 31, 2025, the company has recognized $600,000 of cumulative compensation cost (of $1,800,000 total), the average market price is $60, and there are no employee proceeds. The CFO excludes the RSUs from diluted EPS because employees paid nothing. Evaluate this conclusion.', '["The CFO is correct because RSUs with no exercise price cannot be dilutive", "The CFO is incorrect; RSUs use the treasury stock method with unrecognized compensation cost as assumed proceeds, and the resulting incremental shares should be included if dilutive", "The CFO is incorrect; all 40,000 RSUs must be included in diluted EPS regardless of any proceeds calculation", "The CFO is incorrect; RSUs use the if-converted method, adding all 40,000 shares to the denominator with no numerator adjustment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28A, unvested share-based payment awards are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. The assumed proceeds include unrecognized compensation cost ($1,200,000 remaining). Assumed shares repurchased = $1,200,000 / $60 = 20,000. Incremental shares = 40,000 minus 20,000 = 20,000, which is dilutive. Choice A is incorrect because the treasury stock method still applies—the assumed proceeds are the remaining unamortized compensation cost, not an exercise price.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Treasury stock method always uses average market price
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Kenmore Corp. has 500,000 shares outstanding and 20,000 stock options with an exercise price of $25. During 2025, the average market price was $50 per share. However, Kenmore repurchased 50,000 shares as treasury stock at $55 per share during the year. Under the treasury stock method for diluted EPS, should Kenmore use $50 or $55 to determine the number of shares assumed repurchased from option proceeds?', '["$55, because it reflects the actual cost of reacquiring shares during the period", "$50, but only if the repurchases occurred after the options'' exercise date", "$55, because treasury stock transactions establish the share repurchase price for EPS purposes", "$50, because the treasury stock method always uses the average market price during the period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-23, the treasury stock method assumes that proceeds from the exercise of options are used to repurchase shares at the average market price during the period, regardless of actual treasury stock transactions. Kenmore uses $50 (average market price): proceeds = 20,000 x $25 = $500,000; shares repurchased = $500,000 / $50 = 10,000; incremental shares = 10,000. Choice A is incorrect because the treasury stock method is a hypothetical computation based on average market price, not actual repurchase costs.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Antidilutive convertible bonds exclusion based on per-share impact
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pemberton Inc. has basic EPS of $3.00 (net income $600,000 and 200,000 shares). Pemberton has $500,000 of 12% convertible bonds, convertible into 10,000 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. What is the per-share impact of the convertible bonds, and should they be included in diluted EPS?', '["$6.00 per share; exclude because it exceeds basic EPS", "Per-share impact is $60,000 / 10,000 = $6.00; include because it is above zero", "Per-share impact is $45,000 / 10,000 = $4.50; exclude because it exceeds basic EPS and is therefore antidilutive", "Per-share impact is $45,000 / 10,000 = $4.50; include because all convertible securities enter diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17, convertible securities with a per-share impact exceeding basic EPS are antidilutive and excluded from diluted EPS. Interest add-back = $500,000 x 12% x (1 minus 0.25) = $45,000. Per-share impact = $45,000 / 10,000 = $4.50. Since $4.50 exceeds basic EPS of $3.00, inclusion would increase EPS, making the bonds antidilutive. Choice A incorrectly uses the pre-tax interest of $60,000 instead of the after-tax amount.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Income from continuing operations as control number
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fielding Corp. reports a loss from continuing operations of $100,000 and income from discontinued operations of $250,000, yielding net income of $150,000. Fielding has 100,000 common shares and 5,000 in-the-money stock options. The controller includes the options in diluted EPS because net income is positive. Apply ASC 260 to determine if this treatment is correct.', '["Correct, because net income is positive and the options are in-the-money", "Incorrect; the control number is income from continuing operations, which is a loss, so all potentially dilutive securities must be excluded as antidilutive", "Incorrect; options should only be included in the discontinued operations EPS line item", "Correct, but only for the net income EPS line item and not the continuing operations line"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, income from continuing operations (adjusted for preferred dividends) is the control number for determining whether securities are dilutive. Because Fielding has a loss from continuing operations, including additional shares in the denominator would reduce the loss per share, making the options antidilutive. They must be excluded from diluted EPS for all line items. Choice A is incorrect because net income is not the control number.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Written put options and reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hartley Corp. has 400,000 common shares outstanding and has written put options obligating it to repurchase 20,000 shares at $50 per share. The average market price during 2025 is $40. Under the reverse treasury stock method per ASC 260, how are these written puts treated for diluted EPS?', '["Excluded because put options can never be dilutive", "Included by adding 20,000 shares directly to the denominator", "Excluded because they are out-of-the-money when the exercise price exceeds the market price", "Included by assuming Hartley issues shares at $40 to fund the $50 repurchase, adding 5,000 net incremental shares to the denominator"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options that are in-the-money (exercise price exceeds market price) are dilutive and included using the reverse treasury stock method. Hartley assumes it issues enough shares at the $40 market price to fund the $50 repurchase: $1,000,000 / $40 = 25,000 shares issued, minus 20,000 shares repurchased = 5,000 net incremental shares. Choice C is incorrect because written puts are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the market price, the opposite of call options.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Forward purchase contracts in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Grafton Inc. has 300,000 common shares outstanding and enters a forward contract obligating it to repurchase 15,000 of its own shares at $60 per share on December 31, 2026. The average market price during 2025 is $45. Under ASC 260, how is this forward purchase contract treated for diluted EPS in 2025?', '["Excluded because the settlement date has not yet arrived", "Included by subtracting 15,000 shares from the denominator", "Included using the standard treasury stock method, adding incremental shares only if the exercise price is below the market price", "Included using the reverse treasury stock method, adding 5,000 net incremental shares to the denominator"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, forward purchase contracts to buy back shares are treated similarly to written put options using the reverse treasury stock method when the contract price exceeds the average market price. Grafton assumes it issues shares at $45 to raise $900,000 for the repurchase (20,000 shares), then retires 15,000 shares, adding 5,000 net incremental shares. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 requires inclusion when such contracts are dilutive, regardless of settlement date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Unvested restricted stock excluded from basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On March 1, 2025, Waverly Corp. issues 12,000 shares of restricted stock to employees that vest on December 31, 2027. Waverly has 200,000 unrestricted shares outstanding all year. For basic EPS in 2025, the controller includes 12,000 shares weighted for 10 months (10,000 shares). Apply ASC 260 to determine if this is correct.', '["Incorrect; unvested restricted stock is excluded from basic EPS because the shares are contingently returnable until the vesting condition is met", "Correct; restricted shares are included in basic EPS from the grant date, weighted for the period outstanding", "Incorrect; restricted shares should be included starting from the vesting date, not the grant date", "Correct; all legally issued shares, including unvested restricted stock, must be in the basic EPS denominator"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, unvested restricted stock is excluded from basic EPS because the shares are contingently returnable—if the employee terminates before vesting, the shares are forfeited. Unvested shares are instead included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. The controller incorrectly treated unvested shares as vested. Choice D is incorrect because legal issuance alone does not qualify shares for the basic EPS denominator when forfeiture conditions remain.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Mandatorily redeemable preferred stock and EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Dalton Corp. has 150,000 common shares and 10,000 shares of mandatorily redeemable preferred stock classified as a liability under ASC 480, paying 7% dividends on $100 par value. Net income is $525,000 after deducting the preferred dividends as interest expense. What adjustment, if any, is needed to compute basic EPS?', '["Deduct the $70,000 preferred dividend from the numerator because all preferred dividends reduce EPS", "Add back the $70,000 to the numerator because it was incorrectly classified as interest expense", "No further adjustment is needed because dividends on mandatorily redeemable preferred stock classified as a liability are already reflected as interest expense in net income", "Deduct $70,000 from the numerator and add the 10,000 preferred shares to the denominator"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 480-10-25-4, mandatorily redeemable preferred stock is classified as a liability, and its dividend payments are reported as interest expense, already reducing net income. Since the $70,000 is already deducted, no additional numerator adjustment is required for basic EPS. Choice A is incorrect because deducting again would double-count the preferred dividends.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Full diluted EPS with three securities ranked by per-share impact
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stratton Corp. has net income of $1,000,000, 250,000 common shares, and three potentially dilutive securities: (1) 15,000 stock options with an exercise price of $20 (average market price $50), (2) $600,000 of 5% convertible bonds convertible into 20,000 shares (tax rate 25%), and (3) 10,000 shares of $50 par, 8% convertible preferred stock convertible into 25,000 shares. Rank the securities from most dilutive to least dilutive and determine diluted EPS.', '["Options, Preferred, Bonds; diluted EPS is $3.52", "Bonds, Options, Preferred; diluted EPS is $3.36", "Preferred, Bonds, Options; diluted EPS is $3.36", "Options, Bonds, Preferred; diluted EPS is $3.36"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, securities are ranked by per-share impact: stock options ($0.00), convertible bonds ($22,500 / 20,000 = $1.125), then preferred ($40,000 / 25,000 = $1.60). Basic EPS = ($1,000,000 minus $40,000) / 250,000 = $3.84. Including all three sequentially: ($960,000 + $22,500 + $40,000) / (250,000 + 9,000 + 20,000 + 25,000) = $1,022,500 / 304,000 = $3.36. Choice A uses the correct first security but misstates the remaining order and stops before including preferred.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred if-converted method and basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashbury Corp. has net income of $500,000, 200,000 common shares, and 5,000 shares of $100 par, 8% convertible preferred stock. Each preferred share converts into 4 common shares. For diluted EPS, the controller applies the if-converted method by adding back $40,000 of preferred dividends to the numerator and 20,000 shares to the denominator. What is Ashbury''s basic EPS?', '["$2.50", "$2.30", "$2.27", "$2.73"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, basic EPS deducts preferred dividends from the numerator: ($500,000 minus $40,000) / 200,000 = $2.30. The if-converted adjustments the controller describes apply only to diluted EPS. Choice A ($2.50) incorrectly uses net income without deducting preferred dividends ($500,000 / 200,000 = $2.50). The preferred dividend deduction is required regardless of whether the stock is convertible.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Loss year makes convertible bonds antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northgate Corp. reports a loss from continuing operations of $300,000 with 200,000 common shares. Northgate has convertible bonds that would add $50,000 of after-tax interest to the numerator and 30,000 shares to the denominator. The controller computes diluted EPS as ($300,000 + $50,000) / (200,000 + 30,000) = negative $1.09, compared to basic EPS of negative $1.50. Evaluate this conclusion.', '["Correct; the if-converted method was applied properly and diluted EPS is negative $1.09", "Incorrect; the numerator should not include the interest add-back for a loss year", "Incorrect; the interest add-back should be subtracted, not added, during a loss year", "Incorrect; including the bonds reduces the loss per share from $1.50 to $1.09, which is antidilutive, so diluted EPS must equal basic EPS of negative $1.50"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17, when an entity reports a loss from continuing operations, all potentially dilutive securities are antidilutive because their inclusion reduces the loss per share. Northgate''s basic loss per share is negative $1.50; including the bonds produces negative $1.09, which is less of a loss and therefore antidilutive. Diluted EPS must equal negative $1.50. Choice A fails to recognize that diluted EPS cannot produce a smaller loss per share than basic EPS.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Bonus element in rights offering adjustment factor
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Cypress Corp. has 500,000 shares outstanding and offers a rights issue: one new share for every five shares held at $20 per share. The last trading price before the ex-rights date is $35 per share. Under ASC 260-10-55-17, the theoretical ex-rights price is ($35 x 5 + $20) / 6 = $32.50. What adjustment factor must Cypress apply to retroactively adjust weighted-average shares for pre-rights periods?', '["$35.00 / $32.50 = 1.077", "$32.50 / $35.00 = 0.929", "$20.00 / $35.00 = 0.571", "No adjustment is needed because rights offerings are not treated as stock dividends"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-17, when a rights offering includes a bonus element (the exercise price is below fair value), the weighted-average shares for periods before the rights offering must be multiplied by an adjustment factor equal to fair value per share before the offering divided by theoretical ex-rights price: $35.00 / $32.50 = 1.077. Choice B inverts the ratio, which would incorrectly reduce the prior-period share count.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Contingent shares based on earnings targets in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgeway Corp. agreed in a business combination to issue 50,000 additional shares to the former owners if cumulative net income exceeds $5,000,000 by December 31, 2026. As of December 31, 2025, cumulative net income is $4,800,000, and current-year income is $1,200,000. For diluted EPS in 2025, how should these contingent shares be treated?', '["Excluded because the earnings target has not yet been met", "Included only in the notes to the financial statements", "Included in diluted EPS because current-period earnings, if sustained, would satisfy the target by the end of the contingency period", "Included in basic EPS because the target is virtually certain to be met"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-52, contingently issuable shares based on earnings conditions are included in diluted EPS if the earnings target would be met assuming current-period earnings continue through the contingency period. Ridgeway earned $1,200,000 in 2025, needing only $200,000 more, which is easily achievable if current earnings are sustained. Choice A is incorrect because diluted EPS includes contingent shares when current conditions would satisfy the target.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Share-based payment awards as participating securities under two-class method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornwood Corp. grants 30,000 unvested restricted stock awards that receive nonforfeitable dividends. Net income is $900,000, total dividends declared are $200,000 (of which $20,000 was paid on unvested restricted shares), and there are 270,000 common shares outstanding. The CFO computes basic EPS as ($900,000 minus $20,000) / 270,000 = $3.26. Under the two-class method, evaluate whether this computation is correct.', '["Correct; deducting dividends paid to unvested shares is the proper two-class treatment", "Incorrect; the two-class method only deducts dividends, not undistributed earnings allocated to participating shares", "Incorrect; the unvested shares should be added to the denominator instead of adjusting the numerator", "Incorrect; the two-class method requires allocating both distributed and undistributed earnings to the participating securities based on their participation rights"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method requires allocating both distributed earnings (dividends) and undistributed earnings to each class of participating securities. Undistributed earnings of $700,000 must be allocated proportionally: common receives 270,000/300,000 x $700,000 = $630,000. Basic EPS = ($180,000 + $630,000) / 270,000 = $3.00. Choice A only accounts for dividends and ignores the required allocation of undistributed earnings.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Where EPS must be presented
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under U.S. GAAP, where must public entities present earnings per share data?', '["In the notes to the financial statements only", "On the face of the income statement", "On the statement of stockholders'' equity", "On the statement of cash flows"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, public entities must present basic and diluted EPS on the face of the income statement for income from continuing operations and net income. EPS for discontinued operations may appear on the face or in the notes. Choice A is incorrect because the income statement presentation is mandatory—note disclosure alone is insufficient.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Weighted-average shares definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What does the term weighted-average shares outstanding mean for EPS purposes?', '["The number of common shares outstanding during each portion of the period, weighted by the fraction of the period they were outstanding", "The total shares authorized by the corporate charter", "The sum of shares outstanding at the beginning and end of the period divided by two", "The number of shares outstanding at year-end adjusted for stock splits"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, weighted-average shares outstanding represents the shares outstanding during each portion of the reporting period, weighted by the time fraction each group was outstanding. This accounts for issuances and repurchases at different points in the year. Choice C is incorrect because a simple average of beginning and ending shares does not properly weight mid-period transactions.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Prior-period EPS restatement after stock split
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Kensington Corp. reported basic EPS of $4.00 for 2024 based on net income of $800,000 and 200,000 weighted-average shares. On March 1, 2025, Kensington executed a 2-for-1 stock split. When presenting comparative 2024 EPS in its 2025 financial statements, what amount should Kensington report for 2024 basic EPS?', '["$4.00, the originally reported amount", "$8.00, adjusted upward for the split", "$2.00, retroactively adjusted to reflect the stock split", "$4.00, with disclosure of the split in the notes only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits and stock dividends require retroactive adjustment of all prior-period EPS data presented for comparability. Kensington''s 2024 weighted-average shares are restated from 200,000 to 400,000 (times 2), yielding EPS of $800,000 / 400,000 = $2.00. Choice A is incorrect because presenting the original $4.00 without retroactive adjustment would make prior-period EPS noncomparable with the current period.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: easy / L2 — Identifying a complex capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Marlow Corp. has 500,000 shares of common stock, 50,000 shares of nonconvertible preferred stock, and 20,000 outstanding stock options. Under ASC 260, Marlow has a:', '["Simple capital structure because the preferred stock is nonconvertible", "Complex capital structure because the stock options are potentially dilutive securities", "Simple capital structure because it has only two classes of stock", "Complex capital structure because it has more than 100,000 total shares outstanding"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding potentially dilutive securities. Marlow''s 20,000 stock options qualify as potentially dilutive securities, requiring dual EPS presentation (basic and diluted). Choice A is incorrect because the determination depends on any potentially dilutive security existing, not solely on whether preferred stock is convertible.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Treasury stock method assumption about proceeds
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under the treasury stock method in ASC 260, what are the assumed proceeds from stock option exercises used for?', '["Paying dividends to existing shareholders", "Reducing the company''s outstanding debt", "Funding new capital expenditures", "Repurchasing common shares at the average market price during the period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-23, the treasury stock method assumes that proceeds received from the exercise of stock options are used to repurchase common shares on the open market at the average market price during the period. The difference between shares issued upon exercise and shares assumed repurchased represents the incremental dilutive shares. Choice B is incorrect because the method specifically assumes share repurchase, not debt reduction.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: hard / L3 — Partially paid shares in EPS denominator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Caldwell Corp. has 200,000 fully paid shares outstanding all year and net income of $660,000. On January 1, Caldwell issued 50,000 additional shares at $50 per share under a subscription agreement, but subscribers have paid only $20 per share by year-end. Under ASC 260, what is basic EPS considering the partially paid shares?', '["$3.00, treating partially paid shares as 20,000 equivalent fully paid shares (50,000 x $20/$50)", "$2.64, including all 50,000 partially paid shares at full value in the denominator", "$3.30, excluding all partially paid shares from the denominator until fully paid", "$2.87, treating partially paid shares as 30,000 equivalent shares based on the unpaid fraction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, partially paid shares are included in the weighted-average computation proportional to the amount paid relative to the full subscription price: 50,000 x ($20/$50) = 20,000 equivalent shares. Basic EPS = $660,000 / (200,000 + 20,000) = $3.00. Choice B is incorrect because including all 50,000 shares overstates the denominator since the shares are only 40% paid.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Which entities must report EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which entities are required to present earnings per share?', '["All entities that prepare GAAP financial statements", "Only entities with annual revenues exceeding $10 million", "Public entities with common stock or potential common stock trading in a public market", "Only entities listed on the New York Stock Exchange or NASDAQ"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS disclosure is required for entities that have issued common stock or potential common stock (convertible securities, options, warrants) that trade in a public market. Nonpublic entities are not required to present EPS. Choice A is incorrect because privately held companies preparing GAAP financial statements are exempt from the EPS disclosure requirement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Dual presentation requirement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'When a public entity has a complex capital structure, what EPS presentation is required?', '["Presentation of only diluted EPS on the income statement", "Presentation of both basic and diluted EPS with equal prominence on the face of the income statement", "Presentation of basic EPS on the income statement and diluted EPS in the notes only", "Presentation of basic EPS only, with a narrative description of dilutive securities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, entities with complex capital structures must present both basic and diluted EPS with equal prominence on the face of the income statement for income from continuing operations and net income. Choice A is incorrect because basic EPS must always be presented alongside diluted EPS—an entity cannot report only diluted EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Antidilutive securities definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what are antidilutive securities?', '["Securities whose inclusion in the diluted EPS calculation would increase EPS or decrease the loss per share", "Securities that are convertible into common stock at the holder''s option", "Securities that have been repurchased by the issuing entity as treasury stock", "Securities that reduce the entity''s total number of shares outstanding"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, antidilutive securities are instruments whose inclusion in the diluted EPS computation would increase EPS or decrease the loss per share. ASC 260-10-45-17 requires that antidilutive securities be excluded from diluted EPS. Choice B describes convertible securities generally, which may be either dilutive or antidilutive depending on their per-share impact relative to basic EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Contingent shares with revenue target not met
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fairview Corp. acquired a subsidiary and agreed to issue 40,000 additional shares to the former owners if the subsidiary''s revenue exceeds $8 million in any of the next three years. In 2025, the subsidiary''s revenue is $6.5 million. For basic and diluted EPS in 2025, how are the contingent shares treated?', '["Included in both basic and diluted EPS because the agreement is legally binding", "Excluded from basic EPS because the condition is not met; also excluded from diluted EPS because current-year revenue of $6.5 million, if sustained, would not satisfy the $8 million target", "Excluded from basic EPS but always included in diluted EPS at the full 40,000 shares because the contingency period has not expired", "Included in basic EPS at a proportional 32,500 shares based on $6.5M / $8.0M"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in basic EPS only when all conditions are met. The $8 million revenue target is not satisfied in 2025. For diluted EPS, per ASC 260-10-45-52, shares are included only if current-period results, if sustained, would meet the contingency. Since $6.5 million falls short of $8 million, the shares are excluded from diluted EPS as well. Choice C is incorrect because contingent shares are not automatically included while the contingency period remains open.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
